  "Question": "The equation \\(2^{2x}-8\\cdot2^x+12=0\\) is satisfied by:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\log 3\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\dfrac12\\log 6\\)",
    "C": "\\(1+\\log\\dfrac34\\)",
    "D": "\\(1+\\dfrac{\\log3}{\\log2}\\)",
    "E": "none of these"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "The exponent \\(2x\\) suggests treating \\(2^x\\) as one quantity, because \\(2^{2x}=(2^x)^2\\). Set \\(t=2^x\\), remembering that \\(t>0\\). The equation becomes\n\\[\nt^2-8t+12=0,\n\\qquad (t-2)(t-6)=0.\n\\]\nThus \\(t=2\\) or \\(t=6\\). Both are positive, so both are allowed. Since the exponential and logarithm are inverses, they give exactly\n\\[\nx=1\\quad\\text{or}\\quad x=\\log_2 6.\n\\]\nTo match the second value to the choices, use common logarithms and the product rule:\n\\[\n\\log_2 6\n=\\frac{\\log6}{\\log2}\n=\\frac{\\log2+\\log3}{\\log2}\n=1+\\frac{\\log3}{\\log2}.\n\\]\nHere \\(\\log2\\ne0\\), so the divisions are valid. Both substituted roots reverse to solutions of the original equation, and the factorization leaves no others. The listed solution is therefore \\(\\boxed{1+\\frac{\\log3}{\\log2}}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm D\\).",
